= Inhame (Taro)

#emph[Scope: This entry treats #emph[inhame] (Brazilian Portuguese, in the #emph[northeastern Brazilian usage]) — interpreted here as taro, #emph[Colocasia esculenta] (L.) Schott (Araceae, the #emph[aroid] family — same family as caladium, philodendron, anthurium). The plant is one of the #emph[oldest cultivated foods in human history]: archaeological evidence places domestication in #emph[Southeast Asia / Indo-Burma] at ~7000 BCE or earlier, with independent or parallel cultivation in #emph[South China] and the #emph[Pacific (Oceanic Austronesian)]. NOTE: Brazilian #emph[inhame] terminology is regionally inconsistent — in southern and southeastern Brazilian usage #emph[inhame] can refer to #emph[Dioscorea] (yam, what northeastern Brazil calls cará); this entry follows the northeastern Brazilian register where #emph[inhame = Colocasia esculenta]. See #emph[cara.typ] for #emph[Dioscorea]. Status: classical in Chinese medicine (芋頭 yùtóu / 芋艿 yùnǎi — #emph[Bencao Gangmu]); central in Pacific Polynesian and Hawaiian agricultural-cultural identity (#emph[kalo] in Hawaiian; the #emph[poi] staple); recognized in Sanskrit #emph[kanda-varga] but with species-identification uncertainty; not classical in Tibb or Greco-Roman traditions. The #emph[raw plant contains calcium-oxalate crystals (raphides) and is mildly toxic raw] — must be properly cooked. Brazilian inhame is post-classical extension.]

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin / botanical:* _Colocasia esculenta_ (L.) Schott, family Araceae. The drug-and-food is the *underground corm* (a swollen, vertical, central stem-base — botanically distinct from "tuber" or "root" in the strict sense). Multiple cultivar groups exist with major morphological differences: *dasheen* (large central corm, used as the main food); *eddoe* (smaller corms with multiple side-corms — common in West African and Caribbean cuisine); and many regional Pacific cultivars (*taro proper*, *kalo* in Hawaiian — with extensive Hawaiian-named varieties). The plant is also valued for *young leaves* (the "taro leaves" used in West African callaloo, Hawaiian *lūʻau* leaves, and the Indian *aroideae* leaf-vegetable tradition).

The genus name *Colocasia* from Greek κολοκάσιον (_kolokásion_) — used by Dioscorides for an Egyptian aroid (possibly a lotus or possibly an early-domesticated taro that had reached Egypt). The Egyptian connection is significant: *taro is documented in Egyptian and broader Eastern Mediterranean cultivation by the Hellenistic period*, with Greek-Latin recognition. The species epithet *esculenta* = Latin "edible."#footnote[Etymology and history: Lewis & Short (s.v. _colocasia_); Beekes (s.v. κολοκάσιον); Plucknett, "Taxonomy of the Genus Colocasia," in _Taro: A Review of Colocasia esculenta and Its Potentials_; standard Cucurbita-and-Araceae historical literature. The Mediterranean classical recognition is via Egyptian cultivation; the plant reaches Greek and Roman attention as a foreign Egyptian food.]

*Brazilian Portuguese:* *Inhame* (northeastern Brazilian usage = Colocasia; southern Brazilian usage can = Dioscorea — *regional inconsistency*). The word *inhame* is from the same West African source as the English *yam* (Pulaar / Wolof *nyami* "to eat") — *the Atlantic slave-trade transmission* of West African plant-and-food vocabulary into the Brazilian Portuguese register. The regional inversion of inhame / cará terminology is a notable Brazilian ethnobotanical complication.

*English:* *Taro* — the canonical English name, from Polynesian *taro* (Tahitian, Māori — preserved in modern English usage via 19th-c. Pacific contact); *dasheen* — for the central-corm cultivar; *eddoe* — for the side-corm cultivar; *cocoyam* — for some West African cultivars; *kalo* (Hawaiian).

*Polynesian / Hawaiian:* *Kalo* (Hawaiian); *taro* (most Polynesian languages — Tahitian, Māori, Tongan, Samoan, Cook Islands Māori). *Kalo is the foundational cultural-spiritual food of Hawaiian indigenous tradition* — in the Hawaiian creation narrative (_Kumulipo_), kalo is the *elder brother* of the Hawaiian people, with the first kalo plant (*Hāloa*) being the still-born first child of the sky-father Wākea and earth-mother Hoʻohōkūkalani, from whose burial the kalo plant emerged. The *cultural-spiritual register of kalo in Hawaiian tradition* is comparable to *tila in Hindu ancestor rites* in depth and centrality. *Poi* (mashed cooked kalo, often slightly fermented) is the Hawaiian foundational starch staple, prepared traditionally with daily-fresh pounding and a deep ceremonial valence.#footnote[Hawaiian kalo / Hāloa narrative: standard Hawaiian cultural and religious literature; the elder-brother relationship is central to Hawaiian indigenous identity and to land-management practices. _Kumulipo_ (the Hawaiian creation chant) and modern Hawaiian cultural-historical scholarship.]

*Chinese:* *芋頭 (yùtóu)*, "taro head"; *芋艿 (yùnǎi)*; *芋 (yù)* — the bare character. The herb is *recognized in classical Chinese pharmacopoeia* — appearing in the *Bencao Gangmu* (1578) and in pre-Tang dietetic literature. The Chinese cultivation tradition is ancient (Neolithic Yangtze and Yangshao archaeological evidence).

*Japanese:* *里芋 (satoimo)*, "village taro" — distinguishing from the mountain yam *yamaimo* (Dioscorea, see `cara.typ`). The Japanese small-taro register is preserved in classical kaiseki cuisine.

*Sanskrit:* The classical Sanskrit *कन्द (kanda)* group includes taro-class aroids; specific names include *आर्द्रशृङ्गाटक (ārdraśṛṅgāṭaka)*; *कच्चु (kacchu)* — for some specific taro varieties; the classical identification is uncertain across the kanda-varga literature. Modern Hindi *अरबी (arbī)*, *कच्चू (kacchū)*, *घुइया (ghuiya)* — regional names; modern Bengali *কচু (kachu)*; modern Tamil *செம்பு (cembu)*. The classical Ayurvedic kanda-varga register treats taro-class aroids; specific clinical use is more developed in regional folk-Ayurvedic tradition than in saṃhitā literature.#footnote[Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Kanda-varga_; Sanskrit identification of taro species is uncertain across classical commentary; the modern Indian Ayurvedic tradition (Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_) integrates regional folk identifications.]

*West African names:* *Eddoe*, *cocoyam* (English-influenced West African); *taro* (broadly); various regional African names. Taro is a major staple in much of West and Central Africa, particularly in the high-rainfall coastal and forest zones.

*Arabic / Tibb:* The Egyptian *قُلْقاس (qulqās)* / *colcas* — the Egyptian Arabic name, preserved from the Greek κολοκάσιον; the *colcas in mulukhiya (jute mallow)*, the canonical Egyptian winter stew, is a classical Egyptian-Arab dish. *Modern Avicennan Tibb register*: minor, with the Egyptian regional use being the principal Tibb-adjacent attestation; not a canonical pan-Tibb drug.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

*芋頭 (yùtóu)* is recognized in the *Bencao Gangmu* and earlier Chinese dietetic literature; *not* in the strict Shennong-Bencao top-grade but established in the broader Chinese dietetic-pharmacy register. The clinical use is *Spleen-Stomach harmonizing and nodule-resolving*.

*Property:* neutral (平) to slightly cool (微涼).

*Flavor:* sweet (甘), pungent (辛).

*Channels:* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃).

*Actions:*
- *補中益氣 (bǔ zhōng yì qì)* — tonifies the middle, augments qi.
- *寬腸通便 (kuān cháng tōng biàn)* — eases the intestines, promotes bowel movement.
- *軟堅散結 (ruǎn jiān sàn jié)* — softens hardness, dispels accumulation — for *nodules, lymphadenopathy, scrofula* — paralleling the 昆布 register (see `kombu.typ`); *taro is used in folk Chinese medicine as poultice and internal preparation for benign masses and lymphadenopathy* — the *taro-paste poultice* is a recognized folk-Chinese-medicine application for cold-pattern surface lumps and lymph node swelling.

*Cautions:* the *raw plant is mildly toxic* (calcium-oxalate raphides cause oral irritation and pharyngeal-mucosal irritation); *must be properly cooked* (boiling, steaming, baking) to denature the oxalate and proteinase irritants; the cooking destroys the raw toxicity.#footnote[芋頭 in _Bencao Gangmu_; modern Chinese dietetic and folk-medicine literature; standard taro toxicology on calcium-oxalate raphides.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

Taro is recognized in the broader Ayurvedic kanda-varga but with species-identification uncertainty. The classical reading:
- *Rasa:* madhura (sweet), mild katu (pungent).
- *Guṇa:* guru, snigdha when cooked; rūkṣa-by-some-readings (drying) raw.
- *Vīrya:* śīta to neutral.
- *Vipāka:* madhura.
- *Doṣa effect:* pacifies Vāta and Pitta when cooked; aggravates Kapha at heavy intake.
- *Karma:* _balya_, _bṛṃhaṇa_; mild _hṛdya_; raw form is folk-cautioned as Vāta-aggravating.

Modern Indian usage of *arbī / kacchū / ghuiya* as a vegetable is widespread, particularly in north Indian and Bengali cuisines; classical attestation is via the kanda-varga register.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

*Qulqās (قلقاس) — taro in Egyptian Arab usage* — has *Egyptian classical attestation* (the Greek κολοκάσιον tradition reaching Hellenistic-Egyptian medical literature); modern Egyptian-Arab cuisine preserves the *qulqās bi-l-mulukhiya* (taro and jute mallow) traditional preparation. The pan-Tibb (Avicennan) status is *minor* — Avicenna does discuss colocasia briefly in the Egyptian-foods register but it is not a major drug of the canon. The Egyptian-Coptic / Egyptian-Arab tradition preserves the longest-continuous Mediterranean-Arab taro register.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *芋頭粥 (yùtóu zhōu)* — taro congee; daily dietetic.
- *芋頭排骨湯* — taro and pork rib soup; canonical Chinese family-cooking.
- *芋艿外用糊 (taro paste poultice)* — folk Chinese medicine; for cold-pattern surface lumps and lymphadenopathy; mashed cooked taro applied externally.
- *Dim sum and dessert preparations* — taro cakes (芋頭糕), taro buns (芋頭包), taro paste in sweets.

=== Hawaiian / Polynesian (the canonical cultural register)

- *Poi (Hawaiian)* — *the canonical Hawaiian staple food*: cooked kalo pounded with water to a paste, traditionally allowed to ferment slightly (1–3 days for a sour-pleasant flavor); eaten daily, with deep cultural-spiritual valence. *Poi is the foundational Hawaiian carbohydrate staple* and is invested with central cultural identity meaning.
- *Lūʻau (lau leaves)* — Hawaiian preparation of taro leaves: wrapped with coconut cream, fish, and chicken, baked in an imu (underground oven); canonical Polynesian celebratory preparation.
- *Kalua kalo* — imu-roasted taro.

=== West African / Caribbean

- *Fufu* (in some regional usages — fufu can also be cassava or yam-based); *cocoyam fufu* in some West African regions.
- *Callaloo* (Caribbean) — the leaves of taro (or related aroid Xanthosoma) cooked into a green stew; the canonical Anglophone Caribbean side dish.
- *Eddoe preparations* in various West African and Caribbean traditional cuisines.

=== Egyptian / Levantine

- *Qulqās bi-l-mulukhiya* — Egyptian taro-and-jute-mallow stew; canonical winter dish.
- *Qulqās bi-l-tamatim* — Egyptian taro-with-tomato.

=== Indian (arbī / kacchū / ghuiya)

- *Arbi ki sabzi* — north Indian taro vegetable preparation.
- *Patra / arbi patta* — Gujarati and Maharashtrian rolled-taro-leaf preparation; the canonical Indian use of taro leaves with gram flour, tamarind, and spices.
- *Kacchu / kachu bata* — Bengali taro preparations.

=== Japanese (satoimo)

- *Satoimo no nimono* — simmered Japanese taro; kaiseki classical preparation.
- *Imo nikko* — autumn moon-festival taro preparation (the autumn full-moon tsukimi tradition includes satoimo offerings).

=== Brazilian (inhame, northeastern register)

- *Inhame cozido* — boiled taro; canonical Brazilian breakfast staple in the northeast.
- *Inhame com leite* — taro with milk; a Pernambuco and Bahia traditional preparation.
- *Sopa de inhame* — taro soup; Brazilian and African-Brazilian register.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Taro is a *tropical and subtropical wetland and warm-moist-soil* crop — adapted to *high-rainfall, warm year-round, often wet or paddy-style cultivation*. The plant is *one of the few staple crops that can be grown in flooded or constantly-wet conditions* — the Hawaiian *lo'i* (taro-paddy) and Asian wetland-taro cultivation systems exploit this. The crop is fundamental to *Pacific Polynesian, West African, South and Southeast Asian, southern Chinese, and Caribbean* food systems. See `climates.typ` for the foundational framework.

=== Hot-humid tropical (the canonical staple zone)

The native and canonical growing climate. *Taro as cultural-spiritual staple* in Pacific Polynesia; *taro as daily food* in West Africa, the Caribbean, southern China, and Southeast Asia.

*Cares.* The cooked-sweet-snigdha register is climatically apt for the hot-humid tropical climate; *raw consumption is precluded by the calcium-oxalate toxicity* — proper cooking is constitutive, not optional.

=== Subtropical-humid and continental seasonal-extreme

Cultivated across Japan, Korea, southern China, southern US, southern Italy, Egyptian Nile delta; the seasonal use varies by region.

=== Modern transposition

Modern globalized cuisine has spread taro across all climates; the cooking requirement remains constitutive.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Approximate composition (cooked taro corm, per 100 g):*
- Energy ~142 kcal; Water ~64 g
- Carbohydrate ~35 g (starch predominant; *the starch granules are unusually small*, making cooked taro one of the most easily digestible starches); fiber ~5 g
- Protein ~0.5 g; Fat ~0.1 g
- *Minerals:* potassium ~484 mg, manganese very high, magnesium ~30 mg, copper, zinc
- *Vitamins:* vitamin B6, folate, vitamin C; vitamin E
- *Resistant starch* — modest in cooked taro
- *Calcium-oxalate raphides — in raw form* — the principal toxic compound; *denatured by cooking*
- *Phenolic compounds:* modest; anthocyanins in purple-fleshed cultivars
- *Glycemic index:* low-to-moderate (~50)#footnote[Composition data: USDA FoodData Central; standard Pacific and Asian nutrition data; calcium-oxalate raphide toxicity well-documented.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Easily digestible starch* — the small starch-granule structure makes cooked taro *exceptionally digestible* — the historical basis of its widespread use as *infant first food and convalescent staple* across multiple cultures.
- *Hypoallergenic* — taro is among the *lowest-allergen* staple foods; valuable in pediatric allergy management.
- *Gut microbiome support* — the modest resistant starch fraction supports prebiotic register.
- *Calcium-oxalate raphide toxicity (raw)* — *must be properly cooked*; raw consumption causes oral and pharyngeal irritation; in extreme cases, raw consumption can be dangerous; *cooking is constitutive*.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Culinary register (properly cooked):* unrestricted at normal dietary intake.
- *Raw consumption:* contraindicated; toxic.
- *Allergy:* taro is hypoallergenic; rare allergies reported.
- *Pregnancy:* safe.
- *Infants:* taro is a canonical first-food in multiple culinary traditions (Hawaiian, Polynesian, Chinese, West African); gentle digestibility supports this use.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*

